{
  "question": "What has been proposed to reduce the risk of language models producing messages that demean or intimidate people, and by what mechanism?",
  "reference_answer": "A proposed intervention combines training-data filtering, generation steering, and output filtering. First, training examples marked as toxic by Perspective API are removed, reducing the model’s exposure to such material during training. Second, generation is steered toward less toxic text. Third, a separate fine-tuned BERT classifier detects toxicity in generated text so that flagged outputs can be filtered out. The combination therefore acts on what the model learns from, what it generates, and which outputs pass the final screening. It achieved sixfold and seventeenfold reductions in an automatic toxicity metric for toxic and non-toxic prompts, respectively, compared with the previously reported state of the art. Human evaluation also found reduced toxicity, although automatic scores became less reliable after these interventions.",
  "spans": [
    "Concretely, we find that a combination of i) filtering the LM training data annotated as toxic by [Perspective API](https://perspectiveapi.com/), ii) filtering generated text for toxicity based on a separate, fine-tuned BERT classifier trained to detect toxicity, and iii) [steering](https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02164) the generation towards being less toxic, is highly effective at reducing LM toxicity, as measured by automatic toxicity metrics.",
    "When prompted with toxic (or non-toxic) prompts from the [RealToxicityPrompts](https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.11462) dataset, we see a 6-fold (or 17-fold) reduction compared with the previously reported state-of-the-art, in the aggregate *Probability of Toxicity* metric.",
    "The results of this study indicate that there is a direct and largely monotonic relation between average human and classifier-based results, and LM toxicity reduces according to human judgment.",
    "In addition, we find that automatic evaluation of LM toxicity becomes less reliable once detoxification measures have been applied."
  ]
}
